SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 303 | Tuesday, March 3, 2026

Good morning.

1) The rail merger hearing: board members asked about switching, a lot
Four of the five STB members pressed the applicants on whether they would accept a reciprocal-switching condition at the twelve gateways shipper groups named. The railroads said they would "consider commercially reasonable arrangements," which is not yes. A decision is not due until Q4, but the tone yesterday was cooler than the applicants wanted.

2) Copper closes above $11,400 a tonne
A new record, driven by grid buildouts and data-center demand. Wire and cable lead times are stretching past 30 weeks. If you buy electrical components, this is the input cost to watch this year; it has replaced resin as the number procurement teams complain about first.

3) Meridian Logistics files Chapter 11
The Midwest 3PL ran 4 million square feet across nine buildings and lost two anchor customers in Q4. About 600 jobs. Its customers are being asked to sign new agreements with the stalking-horse bidder by March 20 or find space elsewhere. Freight-broker failures are up 40% year on year and warehouse operators are starting to follow.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 31%. Share of US importers who say they have shifted at least one product line's sourcing out of China since 2023, per a survey out this morning. In 2023 the same survey had it at 12%.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

